<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Jever</h1><p class="st-meta">1359 CE – 1818 CE (approx.) · capital Jever</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Frisian lordship on the North Sea coast, remembered for its last native ruler, Lady Mary, and later held by Catherine the Great.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jeverland was part of the free Frisian lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostringen">Östringen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rustringen">Rüstringen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangerland">Wangerland</a>, where local chieftains (hovetlinge) rose to power in the 14th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-wiemken-the-elder">Edo Wiemken the Elder</a>, active from about 1359, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a> into a lordship, and his descendants resisted the ambitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a> rulers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-east-frisia">County of East Frisia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-jever">Mary of Jever</a>, who ruled from 1517 to 1575, defended her small land against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> by placing it under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> protection as a fief of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>. She extended the castle, granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a> town rights in 1536 and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> farming on the fertile coastal marshes. Childless, she bequeathed Jever to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">County of Oldenburg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a> passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> in 1575, in 1667 to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Anhalt-Zerbst</a>, and in 1793 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a> princess. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and French occupation it was transferred to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-oldenburg">Duchy of Oldenburg</a> in 1818. Jever's castle and brewery remain its landmarks.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-jever.jpg" alt="Lordship of Jever" width="300" height="200"><figcaption>Image: TotaFrisia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JeverFlag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1359 CE – 1818 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jever</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edo Wiemken the Elder, Mary of Jever</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German, Frisian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jeverland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1588&amp;focus=lordship-of-jever" aria-label="Lordship of Jever on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4856 336 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Jever lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5176 592L5183 604M5176 592L5272 638M5176 592L6487 457M5176 592L5183 604"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5183 604h0M5272 638h0M6487 457h0M5183 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5176 592h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5176 592h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jever. <a href="/atlas?year=1588&amp;focus=lordship-of-jever">On the atlas &rarr;</a>